⟦P0⟧Canceling in-progress operations⟦P1⟧You can manage in-progress operations by using Go⟦P2⟧⟦P3⟧⟦P4⟧⟦P5⟧. A ⟦P6⟧ is a⟦P7⟧standard Go data value that can report whether the overall operation it⟦P8⟧represents has been canceled and is no longer needed. By passing a⟦P9⟧⟦P10⟧ across function calls and services in your application, those⟦P11⟧can stop working early and return an error when their processing is no longer⟦P12⟧needed. For more about ⟦P13⟧, see⟦P14⟧⟦P15⟧Go Concurrency Patterns: Context⟦P16⟧.⟦P17⟧For example, you might want to:⟦P18⟧End long-running operations, including database operations that are⟦P19⟧taking too long to complete.⟦P20⟧Propagate cancellation requests from elsewhere, such as when a client⟦P21⟧closes a connection.⟦P22⟧Many APIs for Go developers include methods that take a ⟦P23⟧ argument,⟦P24⟧making it easier for you to use ⟦P25⟧ throughout your application.⟦P26⟧Canceling database operations after a timeout ⟦P27⟧⟦P28⟧You can use a ⟦P29⟧ to set a timeout or deadline after which an operation⟦P30⟧will be canceled. To derive a ⟦P31⟧ with a timeout or deadline, call⟦P32⟧⟦P33⟧⟦P34⟧⟦P35⟧ or⟦P36⟧⟦P37⟧⟦P38⟧⟦P39⟧.⟦P40⟧Code in the following timeout example derives a ⟦P41⟧ and passes it into⟦P42⟧the ⟦P43⟧ ⟦P44⟧⟦P45⟧⟦P46⟧⟦P47⟧method.⟦P48⟧Create a⟦P49⟧with a timeout.⟦P50⟧Pass the timeout⟦P51⟧with a query.⟦P52⟧When one context is derived from an outer context, as ⟦P53⟧ is derived⟦P54⟧from ⟦P55⟧ in this example, if the outer context is canceled, then the derived⟦P56⟧context is automatically canceled as well. For example, in ⟦P57⟧ servers, the⟦P58⟧⟦P59⟧ method returns a context associated with the request.⟦P60⟧That context is canceled if the ⟦P61⟧ client disconnects or cancels the ⟦P62⟧⟦P63⟧request (possible in ⟦P64⟧). Passing an ⟦P65⟧ request’s context to⟦P66⟧⟦P67⟧ above would cause the database query to stop early ⟦P68⟧either⟦P69⟧⟦P70⟧if the overall ⟦P71⟧ request was canceled or if the query took more than five⟦P72⟧seconds.⟦P73⟧⟦P74⟧Note:⟦P75⟧ Always defer a cal